.\" ai-tools-admin(8) -- host administration for Agent Tools Restricted.
.\" Kept in sync with the helper's own usage() text by tests/unit/man.sh, which
.\" compares the command sets of both; @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted at
.\" deploy time (install.sh install_subst / the RPM %prep), like the helper.
.TH AI-TOOLS-ADMIN 8 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
atr, ai-tools-admin \- administer the host a sandboxed coding agent runs on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B atr admin
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENT ]
.br
.B atr admin
.BR \-\-help | \-\-version
.br
.B ai-tools-admin
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ai-tools-admin
administers the host state the sandbox rests on: who is enrolled as an
operator, which optional SELinux policy groups are loaded, the toolchain the
agents run on, which agent binary each session is allowed to start, which shared
skills and subagents each agent loads, and the configuration files an upgrade
leaves to reconcile.
Every command runs as root and refuses any other caller, which is what
separates it from
.BR ai-tools (1)
\(em the project-lifecycle CLI, run as your own login user, which refuses root
for every command that writes an operator's registries.
.PP
.B "atr admin COMMAND"
runs
.B ai-tools-admin
at its fixed path, directly as root and otherwise through
.B sudo
after one line on standard error saying so, and every example on this page is
spelled that way.
Type it without a leading
.BR sudo :
sudo then authorizes
.BR /usr/local/libexec/ai-tools/ai-tools-admin ,
the command a grant for this program names, while
.B "sudo atr admin"
has sudo authorize
.BR /usr/sbin/atr ,
which a rule naming the program alone does not cover.
.PP
A command is a bare word and
.B \-\-
introduces an option, so a collection takes its verb after the noun
.RB ( "operators add" ,
.BR "selinux groups enable" ).
A collection named on its own lists what it holds.
A domain named on its own
.RB ( selinux ,
.BR system )
prints its commands, since every verb it owns changes the host.
.PP
Commands that write a file back it up first, name each path they touched, and
state each yes/no question's default in the prompt
.RB ( "[Y/n] (default: Yes)" );
plain Enter \(em and any run without a terminal \(em takes that default.
.SS Operators
An operator is a login user, a person or a rootless service account, that
drives the sandbox through the shared
.B ai-tools
account.
Enrolment writes two facts:
a name in
.B OPERATORS
in
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5),
from which the root helpers resolve who owns a path, and membership of the
.B ai-ops
group, which the
.BR sudoers (5)
grant and the launch wrapper both gate on.
.PP
Claiming a project needs a third fact this command cannot write: a general
.BR sudo (8)
grant, which the host's own sudoers decides.
.B "operators add"
reports which of the two shapes the enrolment produced \(em an account that
can claim projects as well as launch sessions, or one that launches sessions
on projects another operator claimed for it with
.BR "atr projects claim \-\-for" .
.PP
Group membership applies to new login sessions, so an operator enrolled from
an already-open shell starts a new one before launching an agent.
.SS SELinux groups
The core policy module confines every session to the
.B ai_tools_t
domain and covers a repository workload: files, git, HTTPS.
The optional groups widen that domain for one workload each, are disabled by
default, and are loaded per host.
.B "selinux groups enable"
loads the compiled module the
.B ai-tools-selinux
package ships, needing no source tree and no
.BR selinux\-policy\-devel .
.PP
Only groups marked
.I stable
are shipped.
A group marked
.I experimental
is an unaudited draft and is refused here, with a pointer to the source
checkout that compiles and verifies it.
.SS The toolchain
The agents run on a Node toolchain owned by the sandbox account, under
.IR /opt/ai\-tools .
.B "system bootstrap"
installs it: the account and its home if they are absent, then nvm, Node, and
each enabled agent's npm package.
It is the first command to run on a new host, and the one that installs
software over the network \(em which is why no install scriptlet runs it for
you: a scriptlet has to succeed offline and inside a build chroot.
.PP
No agent ships enabled.
On a host whose
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5)
does not yet name one, the command asks which one installed agent to enable
and writes
.B AI_TOOLS_AGENTS
for it before it installs anything;
.B \-\-agents
makes that choice without asking.
A run with no terminal and no
.B \-\-agents
installs Node alone and names the line to set.
.PP
It is idempotent, so it is also the command to re-run after enabling another
agent in
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5):
an existing account, nvm install or Node version is reused rather than rebuilt.
A scheduled timer maintains the toolchain afterwards.
.PP
A bare run provisions the toolchain and the agents, which is what a first host
needs.
.B "\-\-scope full"
goes on to run the setup of every enabled integration, each through the domain
it contributes, so provisioning a host that also runs .NET is one command
rather than one per layer.
.SS Commands a provider package contributes
.B ai-tools-admin
ships in the base package, which is installed before a host knows which agent and
integration packages it will add, so the documented command set is not the whole
surface.
A provider package contributes a domain named for itself \(em an executable it
drops in
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/admin-commands.d
\(em and this command discovers it.
The
.B dotnet
domain works this way:
.B "dotnet bootstrap"
creates and labels the sandbox-side .NET state,
.B "dotnet tools install"
adds shared global tools, and
.B "dotnet status"
reports the host SDKs and what the sandbox has.
They exist on a host that installed
.BR ai\-tools\-integration\-dotnet ,
and not on one that did not.
.PP
.B \-\-help
lists the domains this host has, with a line describing each, so what it prints
and what dispatches are one list.
.IB DOMAIN " \-\-help"
prints that domain's own commands.
.PP
Installing a provider is what makes its commands exist; enabling it in
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5)
is what gives a session the provider itself.
.PP
Two things are checked before any of them runs.
A contributed command must be root-owned and writable by neither group nor
other, as must the directory holding it; one file failing that refuses every
contributed command, since only root may write there and a file that is not
root's alone is one something else can rewrite between runs.
A packaged command is installed in that state, so reinstalling the package that
owns it \(em
.B rpm \-qf
names it \(em is the remedy, together with finding out how the file changed;
re-permissioning it in place is not.
.PP
Each command must also declare the domain it is installed as, the least
version of this interface it needs, and the verbs it answers.
A file that declares none of this is not run as a command, and one needing a
newer interface than this
.B ai-tools-admin
implements is refused naming both versions rather than run in a state neither
package can explain.
A command claiming a name this page documents is refused rather than merged.
Every refusal is reported and leaves the command surface smaller.
.SS Entrypoints
An entrypoint is the agent binary a session execs.
A toolchain update installs a fresh one, which leaves it carrying the wrong
SELinux label, so the domain transition stops firing and the next launch
refuses rather than running the session unconfined.
.PP
The reconcile that clears this runs on its own after an update, through the
.B ai-tools-relabel.path
watcher and the agent package's install scriptlet.
.B "system entrypoints relabel"
is the command an administrator runs when neither did \(em an out-of-band
upgrade, or a watcher that was down \(em and when either
.B status
or
.B "atr status"
reports an entrypoint unverified or not labelled.
.B status
answers from the labels themselves, where
.B "atr status"
answers from the last reconciliation's record.
.SS Layout
Every path this stack installs or creates is one row of a table the base
package ships, with the owner, the mode, the ACL and, where the policy
declares one, the SELinux type the row carries.
.B "system layout"
prints that table as the host carries it now and names each field that
drifted;
.B "system layout reconcile"
brings each entry back to its row and logs every change.
The reconcile also runs unattended, so a mode an upgrade or a hand edit
changed is restored without an operator step; its entry under
.B COMMANDS
names each caller.
The command assumes this executable and the libraries beside it are the ones
the package installed; a host where that does not hold is repaired by
reinstalling the package or redeploying from source.
A row whose file gates a session (a provider manifest,
.IR operator.conf ,
the shim) is reported and converged like any other: no launch reads this
table, and the gate that refuses a session is that file's own reader, in the
process making the decision.
A package is read as installed while either of two files it ships exists;
with both gone its rows leave the table, which
.B "rpm \-Va"
still reports.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B operators
List the enrolled operators, one per line.
This is what a bare
.B operators
runs, and
.B "operators list"
is the same command spelled in full.
.TP
.B operators add \fR[\fIUSER\fR]
Enrol
.IR USER ,
defaulting to
.BR $SUDO_USER .
Seeds that account's own config first \(em an allowlist at
.I ~/.config/ai\-tools/allowed\-projects
and secret\-name patterns at
.IR ~/.config/ai\-tools/secret\-patterns ,
each carrying its format header alone \(em then appends the name to
.BR OPERATORS ,
adds the account to
.BR ai\-ops ,
enables
.BR loginctl (1)
linger for the sandbox account, and settles where that account's shell finds an
agent launcher.
.RS
.PP
That last step is the one an operator is asked about. It reads, from a login
shell of the account, which
.B claude
a shell there would run: the sandbox wrapper in
.IR /usr/local/bin ,
or an agent installed elsewhere on that PATH \(em which starts unconfined, as
that operator, outside every control this stack applies. The question states
which of the two the account gets today and offers to source the PATH ordering
fragment from the account's
.I ~/.bashrc
and
.IR ~/.bash_profile ,
after the nvm init. Declining an account that already reaches an agent outside
.I /usr/local/bin
is reported as a warning naming the binary that wins, not passed over in
silence, and the line to add is printed either way.
.PP
It also repoints a guard line that names the fragment's former path, which an
upgrade would otherwise leave sourcing a file that no longer exists. That
rewrites one path inside a line this stack wrote and leaves the rest of the file
as it was. The rename does not change what the line does, so it does not write
a backup.
.RE
An account whose home has no
.I ~/.config
yet is asked about first, default
.BR Yes ,
and the directory is created with the host's umask deciding its mode; the
ai\-tools directory inside it is
.B 700
and its files
.BR 600 .
A first enrolment that cannot write the allowlist is refused with the host
unchanged, since an operator without one can neither start a session nor claim
a project.
Idempotent: a re-run reconciles each of those and reports what was already in
place, and exits non\-zero when the config is still missing.
Refuses root and the sandbox account.
.TP
.B operators remove \fIUSER\fR
Withdraw
.IR USER 's
enrolment: drops the name from
.B OPERATORS
and the account from
.BR ai\-ops .
Leaves the account's own allowlist and configuration on disk, so re-enrolling
restores the projects it had.
.TP
.B selinux groups
Report the core module and every optional group with its state
.RB ( [LOADED] / [disabled] ),
its stability, and what it covers.
Reports that there is no policy to manage when SELinux is disabled.
.TP
.B selinux groups enable \fIGROUP\fR...
Load the shipped module for each
.IR GROUP .
Several names load in turn, so the set a toolchain declares
.RB ( ai-tools-providers (5))
is one command; every name is checked before any is loaded.
Prints the group list when a
.I GROUP
is not a registered group, and refuses an
.I experimental
group with the source-checkout command that compiles it.
A module a group was loaded under before it was renamed is replaced in the
same step.
Loading a group widens what SELinux permits and does not lift the session's
namespace filter.
.TP
.B selinux groups disable \fIGROUP\fR
Unload the module for
.IR GROUP .
Works for any loaded group, stable or experimental.
.TP
.B system bootstrap \fR[\fB\-\-scope full\fR] [\fB\-\-agents \fINAMES\fR]
Provision the sandbox account and the toolchain the enabled agents run on.
Decides first which agents that is: where
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5)
does not name any, it lists every installed agent and asks which one to enable,
writes
.B AI_TOOLS_AGENTS
for the answer, and provisions what it wrote; a menu left unanswered, or
answered with none, installs Node alone and names the line to set, at exit 0.
A line already present is not asked about; one naming more than one agent is
answered with a notice stating what those agents share.
Next, still before the network is reached, removes from the toolchain the
package of every installed agent that line does not name, and its launcher
with it: no session of any agent starts while such a package is present, and
this command is what the refusal names.
The agent's own state under
.I /opt/ai-tools
stays, and the run says so.
Then creates the
.B ai-tools
account and its
.I /opt/ai-tools
home when they are absent, installs nvm and Node as that account, installs each
agent enabled in
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5),
points each launcher at the binary it installed, and enables the update timer.
On a host where SELinux is enforcing and the
.B ai_tools
module is loaded, offers to require both at every launch
.RB ( AI_TOOLS_REQUIRE_SELINUX
and
.B AI_TOOLS_REQUIRE_ENTRYPOINT_VERIFY
in
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5)),
defaulting to yes, including without a terminal; a key already present is not
asked about.
Offers to set the sandbox account's git commit identity, keeping the seeded
default without a terminal.
.B Reaches the network,
for the nvm installer and the npm packages.
Idempotent: a re-run reuses an existing account, nvm install and Node version,
and provisions whatever is newly enabled.
Which agents it installs comes from
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5)
and the installed manifests, so it names none of them itself.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-agents " "NAME\fR[,\fP NAME\fR...]\fP"
Enable the named agents without asking.
Each is written
.BI agent\- NAME\fR,\fP
.I NAME
being a manifest basename under
.IR /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/agents.d ,
and lands in the file in that form; the bare
.I NAME
is accepted too.
Each is checked against the installed manifests before the line is written, and a name
with no manifest refuses the run with the file unchanged.
More than one name is accepted and draws the same notice a hand-written line
naming several does.
The unattended form; a value is required.
.TP
.BR \-\-scope " " minimal | full
How far to provision.
.B minimal
\(em the default, and what a bare
.B "system bootstrap"
does \(em is the toolchain and the enabled agents.
.B full
additionally runs the
.B bootstrap
of every integration enabled in
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5),
through the domain each one contributes, so a host is provisioned end to end in
one command.
Each integration is attempted and every failure named before the command exits
non-zero; an enabled integration that does not contribute a
.B bootstrap
is reported and skipped.
.RE
.TP
.B system entrypoints relabel
Reconcile every enabled agent's entrypoint, in two steps.
It verifies the binary against the checksum its vendor signed and records the
result in that agent's pin, which the launch compares the binary against; then
it restores the entrypoint's
.B ai_tools_exec_t
SELinux label.
The verification needs the host online and
.B fails soft
when it cannot reach the vendor, so a disconnected host still gets its label
back; a checksum
.B mismatch
fails the command, an entrypoint that is not the binary its vendor published
being a more serious finding than any label.
This command always re-fetches the vendor's signed manifest, where the
unattended callers may answer from an unchanged pin.
Takes no argument: the entrypoints come from the installed agent manifests.
See
.I /usr/share/doc/ai\-tools/docs/system/entrypoint\-verification.md
and
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5).
.TP
.B system post-upgrade \fR[\fB\-\-check \fR[\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-format tsv\fR]]
Reconcile the baseline copies left beside the configuration files this stack
owns: the
.I FILE.rpmnew
an rpm upgrade parks, and the
.I FILE.DATE\-N.shipped
a from-source install leaves beside a kept file that differs from its copy.
Where a file has a copy of each kind, the newer one by modification time is
the reference, and each file's block names which route left it.
A file or copy dated after the system clock means the clock is behind, so the
command names the clock as the first thing to set and does not compare
a file until it is.
Each file gets the treatment its content calls for: the agent's
.I settings.json
has the hook declarations it lacks merged in, with its permission rules left
as written;
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5)
is reported, its provider lists aside;
.I /etc/sudoers.d/ai\-tools
is shown only.
A copy under
.IR /etc/ai\-tools ,
.IR /etc/codex ,
or an agent's configuration directory that none of these names is reported
too: a
.I *.conf
file as
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5)
is, anything else by the
.B diff
command that compares it.
A reported configuration file is never printed, since one may hold a
credential; the report names the options it lacks, the keys you set, and
whether the comments differ.
Its merge command opens your file beside its
.I .rpmnew\-merge
copy, the package copy with every value your file sets carried in, written
readable by root alone beside the
.IR .rpmnew ,
so the comparison shows what the new version changed rather than each value
you chose.
It is removed once no merge is pending for it; a merge copy another account
owns is left as it is.
A copy that differs only in your values is offered for removal without
a merge.
The merge confirms and writes a dated backup before changing anything.
Each file closes by naming its reference copy
as yours to delete, and prints the command that deletes it only when your
file mentions every option the copy documents and carries the same comments.
A copy dated before the installation is named as an earlier version's
template, and the dated
.I .bak
and
.I .shipped
copies kept beside the configuration files are listed.
Whether or not a copy is waiting, the command rewrites each provider list in
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5)
that an earlier release wrote with bare names:
.B claude\-code
in
.B AI_TOOLS_AGENTS
becomes
.BR agent\-claude\-code ,
and
.B core
in
.B AI_TOOLS_FILTERS
becomes
.BR filter\-base .
It writes a dated backup first, rewrites a key only when every name it holds
is installed, and leaves a key holding any other name as written, naming it;
until each list is rewritten no session starts.
Once it rewrites
.BR AI_TOOLS_AGENTS ,
it reconciles the entrypoints of the agents that line now enables, as
.B system entrypoints relabel
does.
This is the one change it makes to that file, and the unattended run makes it
too.
Whether or not a copy is waiting, the command also names each
.B permissions.ask
entry the agent's
.I settings.json
lacks for an installed command that sends data off the host, with the line
to add; it does not write that entry.
Likewise it names each key an agent's managed configuration file, such as
.IR /etc/codex/requirements.toml ,
does not set that the copy the package ships under
.I /usr/share/ai-tools
does, with the commands that merge the file with its package copy side by
side, the new file on the right, in
.BR meld (1)
or
.BR vimdiff (1)
through
.BR sudoedit (8),
so the merge tool runs as you rather than as root; where no
.I .rpmnew
is waiting, the first command recreates it from the shipped copy.
It does not edit the file.
Idempotent, and leaves every file as it is when no baseline copy
is waiting.
Exits 0 when no file is left to act on, 4 while one is, and 1 when a merge it
was asked to make failed; a copy identical to its file counts as clean.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Write the findings without asking or writing, as the record stream
.BR ai-tools-records (5)
states: a header row, then one tab-separated row per finding, and no output
at all on a host with no finding.
Exits 0 with no row, 4 when a row needs attention, and 5 when a source could
not be read, so a
.BR cron (8)
job mails only a host that needs you.
Each row's
.I subject
is the file or directory the finding is about,
.I item
names what inside it the row is about (a hook's event and command, a
permission list and rule, a key, a provider list's key and name) and is empty
where the subject is the whole finding, and
.I detail
is display prose.
.I subject\-type
is
.B file
for a configuration file, and for an asset the type of what is at its path, or
the type expected there where no entry is.
No row carries an
.IR operator :
every subject is host-wide.
A finding that needs attention makes the command exit 4:
.B hook\-missing
and
.B hook\-repeated
(a hook declaration the merge would add or remove),
.B rule\-missing
(a permission rule the package copy of
.I settings.json
carries that the file does not),
.B ask\-missing
(a
.B permissions.ask
entry the file lacks),
.B key\-missing
(a key a managed configuration file does not set that its shipped copy does),
.B option\-unmentioned
(an option the copy documents that the file does not mention),
.B rpmnew\-differs
(a difference to carry over by hand),
.B rpmnew\-review
(the sudoers grant changed),
.B rpmnew\-orphan
(a package copy whose file is gone),
.B asset\-missing
(a shipped skill, subagent, or orientation text not seeded),
.B asset\-unlinked
(an agent without its link to one),
.B list\-unmigrated
(a provider list the command would rewrite, with the old and the new names as
.IR DETAIL ),
.B list\-unmigratable
(a name in a provider list that does not match any installed manifest or rule
set, to edit by hand).
An
.B error
row (a check that could not run, or a collector that failed, with the check
named as
.I ITEM
and the reason as
.IR DETAIL )
makes it exit 5: the rows before it stay valid, and the reading is
incomplete.
With none of these the command prints nothing and exits 0.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Also list the findings that need no action, without changing the exit status:
.B rpmnew\-residual
(a package copy identical to its file),
.B copy\-kept
(a dated
.IR .bak ,
.IR .shipped ,
or
.I .retired
copy),
.B asset\-outdated
(a live asset older than the shipped one), and
.B asset\-overridden
(your own file where a shipped asset or its link would be).
.TP
.B \-\-format tsv
The stream format, and the default.
.RE
.TP
.B system layout \fR[\fBlist\fR] [\fB\-\-check \fR[\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-format tsv\fR]]
Print every entry the layout table declares for the packages installed on
this host, one line each: what drifted, the path, the subject type, the
owner, the mode, the named ACL entries and the SELinux type.
Every attribute column is the value read now; a drifted cell carries the
declared value in brackets.
The entries are the shipped paths under
.IR /usr/local ,
.IR /etc/ai\-tools ,
.IR /opt/ai\-tools ,
.IR /var/opt/ai\-tools ,
.I /var/log/ai\-tools
and the unit directories, each installed agent's configuration directory,
and the configuration directory of each enrolled operator, printed with the
operator in front.
A manual page row is the compressed file where one exists.
The content of a package file is compared with its package through
.BR rpm (8)
and reads as unverified where
.B rpm
is absent or the package was installed from source.
Exits 0 when every entry is at its row, 4 when one drifted, is missing, is of
another type or holds other content, and 5 when a field or an input could not
be read.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Write the findings as the record stream
.BR ai-tools-records (5)
states, and no output on a converged host.
Each row's
.I subject
is the path,
.I subject\-type
the type read there or the declared type where no entry is,
.I operator
the account for an operator's row,
.I item
the field with its declared and its read value
.RB ( "mode 0644 0600" ),
and
.I detail
display prose carrying, for a state the reconcile does not repair, the
remedy.
The findings
.BR owner\-drift ,
.BR mode\-drift ,
.BR acl\-drift ,
.BR label\-drift ,
.B content\-drift
(the file's bytes are not the package's),
.B missing
and
.B type\-mismatch
each exit 4;
.B unreadable
(a field whose reading did not complete) and
.B error
(an input the enumeration could not read, or a reader that refused the entry)
exit 5.
A package file
.BR rpm (8)
records as not installed or as net shared, as an install with
.B tsflags=nodocs
records a documentation file it leaves out, is not a finding while it is
absent.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Also write an
.B ok
row per entry at its row, without changing the exit status.
.TP
.B \-\-format tsv
The stream format, and the default.
.RE
.TP
.B system layout reconcile \fR[\fB\-\-without\-rpm\fR]
Bring every entry to its row: the owner, the ACL and the mode through a
descriptor pinned to the entry the read observed, then the SELinux type
through
.BR restorecon (8)
without
.BR \-F .
A missing directory is created under an existing parent.
The states it does not repair are each printed as a refused line naming the
remedy: a package file whose content differs from its package or could not be
verified, a regular file at another owner or writable by an account its row
does not name (a package file reinstalled; any other file reviewed and
restored by hand), a missing file or link, an entry of another
type where a directory or a file belongs, and a row under an ancestor that is
not at its row after its own step.
Each change is one line in the journal under the tag
.B ai\-tools\-layout
and in
.IR /var/log/ai\-tools/layout.log ,
at notice where it widens access and at info otherwise.
Runs are serialized on
.IR /run/lock/ai\-tools\-layout.lock ;
a lock the command cannot take within a minute is an error and no write.
A run that has not finished within ten minutes is stopped and reported as an
error at exit 1, so a run blocked on an entry does not hold the lock.
Exits 0 with every entry at its row, 1 when a write failed or the read
after a repair found it did not hold, 5 when an observation was incomplete, and 4 while a refused state
remains, each after every entry it could act on.
The unattended callers ignore the status: an upgrade, a source install, the
provisioning run and
.B ai\-tools\-relabel.service
each run it, the last ahead of the relabel helper, whose result stays the
unit's.
A host's own adjustment runs after it through a drop-in,
.IR /etc/systemd/system/ai\-tools\-relabel.service.d/local.conf ,
holding
.B Wants=
for a unit ordered
.BR After=ai\-tools\-relabel.service ;
a direct run of this command, an install and a bootstrap do not start that
unit, so a value it applies waits for the next relabel or a
.BR systemctl (1)
start.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-without\-rpm
Skip the content comparison: every package file reads as unverified, and a
drifted one is reported, not repaired.
The transaction trigger passes it, since the rpm database is held while a
transaction runs; the relabel the transaction starts runs the full pass.
.RE
.TP
.B assets
Print the state of every entry of
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSETS
and each link a reconcile would place, repoint or remove, without changing
anything.
Each pending change is a record of its own, which
.BR ai-tools-assets (5)
describes.
This is what a bare
.B assets
runs, and
.B "assets list"
is the same command spelled in full.
It reads without the assets lock, as
.B status
does, so it answers while a reconcile runs.
It exits 0 when the view and every agent's links match the line, 4 while
a change is pending or an entry needs attention, and 5 when a reading could
not be made; it does not write, so it does not exit 1.
.TP
.B assets enable \fIIDENTIFIER\fR...
Enable each asset, written
.IB SET / KIND / NAME
as in
.BR AI_TOOLS_ASSETS ,
then run
.BR "assets reconcile" .
The line replaces the file's own
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSETS
line or the commented default under its header; a file carrying neither, one
written before the key, takes it at its end, and the command says so, since
.B system post-upgrade
is what carries the line under its header from the shipped template.
Every
.I IDENTIFIER
is checked before anything is written, and the first that fails refuses the
whole call with
.B operator.conf
as it was: one outside the grammar, a
.I KIND
other than
.B skills
or
.BR subagents ,
and a set no binding names.
An asset whose set is not installed yet is accepted where its set is bound,
and is reported
.B set\-absent
until its package arrives.
Each new entry is added once, and the line is written sorted by kind, then
set, then name, so an order written by hand is replaced by the next
.B enable
or
.B disable
that changes the line; a call whose every asset is already listed leaves the
file as it is.
Before a write,
.I operator.conf
is backed up to a dated
.I .bak
beside it.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-set " SET"
Enable instead every asset
.I SET
holds now that passes the rules base enforces, one entry each, then reconcile.
A set the reconcile would refuse as a whole \(em absent, unbound, untrusted,
tampered, unverified, invalid, or requiring a capability this release does
not define \(em refuses the call with its reason; one that needs a newer
base, a profile an enabled agent lacks, or an integration that is off is
written, and the reconcile reports it.
An asset that fails a rule is reported and left out.
A later release of the set does not enable an asset it adds: enable that one by
name.
.RE
.TP
.B assets disable \fIIDENTIFIER\fR...
Remove each asset from
.BR AI_TOOLS_ASSETS ,
back the file up, and reconcile, so the asset is gone from the view and from
every agent's directory when the command returns.
A session already running keeps what it loaded.
An
.I IDENTIFIER
the line does not hold is reported
.B not\-enabled
and does not stop the others.
.TP
.B assets reconcile
Bring the view under
.I /opt/ai\-tools/skills
and
.I /opt/ai\-tools/subagents
and each enabled agent's links into it up to date with
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSETS
and the sets installed now: one link per enabled asset that is installed,
verified against its set's signature and valid, and no other.
It does not take an argument and runs after every package change to a set, so it is
the command to run after editing the line by hand.
.RS
.PP
Each command that writes \(em
.BR enable ,
.B disable
and
.B reconcile
\(em takes the assets lock before it reads
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSETS
and holds it to its end, so two of them at once each read the line the other
wrote; one that waits more than two minutes for the lock exits 1 with the line
and the view as they were.
An
.I operator.conf
that could not be read whole, and a list to write that does not hold exactly
the entries read with this call's change, refuse the call with the file
unchanged.
A write that could not be confirmed exits 1: the file holds the list it held or
the one the call wrote, and the message names the backup and
.BR "atr admin assets reconcile" .
Each writes one record per entry of
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSETS
\(em
.B linked
or the reason it is not \(em and one per link it placed or removed, in the
stream
.BR ai-tools-records (5)
states.
The records are sorted by kind, then set, then name, so the rows for one asset
are adjacent: the change to
.BR AI_TOOLS_ASSETS ,
the entry, the link in the view, then each agent's link.
Each reason, and what to do about it, is in
.BR ai-tools-assets (5).
A link is placed only where the name is free or holds an earlier link of these commands:
an entry of your own at an asset's name, in the view or in an agent's
directory, is left as it is and reported.
.RE
.TP
.B status
Report this host's health: the installed version, whether the toolchain is
provisioned, each managed
.BR systemd (1)
unit, each asset
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSETS
enables that is not linked with its reason, each cache the sandbox account
cleans with its last cleanup and the size it holds now, the sandbox
account's own unit search path, and \(em per enabled agent
\(em both halves of the entrypoint reconciliation: whether the binary matches
the checksum pinned for it, and what SELinux type its paths carry.
Where SELinux is active it closes with an SELinux status section, the checks
a launch makes under
.BR AI_TOOLS_REQUIRE_SELINUX :
whether
.B ai_tools_t
is a permissive domain, and each Boolean that widens it with the value
a launch requires, marked launch blocked where the requirement is in force and
not enforced where it is not.
.RS
.PP
It is the same report
.B "atr status"
gives an operator, completed with the readings that vantage point lacks
the privilege for and prints as
.BR ? :
the sandbox account's own
.B "systemd \-\-user"
units, read live over the machine transport; the entrypoint pin, which sits in
a state directory a non-operator cannot traverse; whether the installed binary
still matches that pin, hashed inside a toolchain only root can enter; the
SELinux type each agent path carries there; and that account's systemd unit
search path.
Every verdict comes from the registry both reports read, so the two differ in
what each is allowed to see and never in what either says about the same unit.
An update run that could not reach the package registry reads SKIPPED here too,
as
.BR ai-tools (1)
describes.
.PP
A
.B ?
line names why the state is unknown: there is no
.BR systemctl ,
a oneshot unit has not run since boot (its
.B .path
watcher starts it on the next change, so the line does not name a command),
the state could not be read, with the command that shows it, or neither the
sandbox account's manager nor its last-run stamp could be read.
.PP
In the
.B Caches
section,
.B FAILED
and
.B LINKS
need attention, and the line under each names the remedy;
.B OVERDUE
is informational and does not count.
.PP
In the
.B "Sandbox unit search path"
section,
.B DRIFTED
and
.B UNEXPECTED
need attention: re-run
.B "atr admin system bootstrap"
for the first, and inspect and remove the named entry for the second.
.B FUTURE
means the update timer's stamp is dated ahead of the clock, so a missed update
does not get a catch-up run; correct the clock and restart the timer.
.PP
In the
.B Assets
section,
.B successor\-unenabled
needs attention: an upgrade moved assets this package no longer ships to
.IR /opt/ai\-tools/retired ,
their successors in an asset set are not enabled, and
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSETS
is absent from
.IR /etc/ai\-tools/operator.conf .
The line names the withdrawn assets and the set; the line under it names the
commands that end it:
.RS
.PP
.EX
sudo dnf install ai-tools-assets-sets-core
atr admin assets enable --set core
.EE
.RE
.PP
or, to keep the set off,
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSETS=[]
in
.IR operator.conf .
Writing the key, as a list or as
.BR [] ,
ends the line; a successor that is listed and does not link is reported by its
own line instead.
.PP
The
.B Assets
section ends with a summary line when it holds a finding:
.RS
.PP
.EX
Summary: 1 finding needs attention in this section (exit 4)
Summary: 2 findings need attention and 1 reading is unavailable in this section (exit 5)
.EE
.RE
.PP
It names the counts that are not zero and the exit status the section sets.
A section without a finding does not print it.
The line is for a person reading the report; a monitor reads the exit status.
.PP
The section's OK line counts the assets
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSETS
enables and those that resolved to linked.
An asset resolves while its link is still missing, and
.B "atr admin assets list"
names the links a reconcile would place.
.PP
The label reading is the one this command alone can give.
.B "atr status"
reports what the last reconciliation
.IR achieved ,
an event that may be hours old; this reports the type the file carries now, so
a label that has drifted since \(em an out-of-band
.BR restorecon ,
a package that reinstalled the binary \(em is visible without running the
reconcile.
.PP
Read-only: it reads the labels and does not relabel, register a file-context
rule, or take the policy-store lock, so it does not change the state it reports
on.
It does not take an argument.
Exits 4 when anything needs attention and 5 when a library the package ships did
not load, so a section could not make a reading it promises (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" );
either way it runs from a monitor or a
.BR cron (8)
job without parsing its output. A
.B ?
line does not change the exit, as in
.BR ai-tools (1).
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
Both options read no host state and leave the host as it is, so both answer any caller
rather than only root, and each ignores any further argument.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the command summary and exit. The bare word
.B help
in the command position prints the same summary, and no provider package can
contribute a domain of that name.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the installed version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed.
An idempotent command that found no work left lands here, as does a
command with no policy to manage because SELinux is disabled.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed; for
.B "system layout reconcile"
a write returned non-zero, or the read after a repair found a field that
did not take, a field that drifted meanwhile, or the entry gone from its path.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was rejected \(em an unknown command, a missing argument, or
a domain named with no command after it.
.TP
.B 4
A report found something that needs attention and read every source
.RB ( status ", the " assets " verbs, " "system post\-upgrade"
with or without
.BR \-\-check ,
and the
.B "system layout"
commands, for which a refused state remaining is the finding).
.TP
.B 5
A report could not read one of its sources, so its findings are incomplete
.RB ( status ,
the
.B assets
commands,
.BR "system post\-upgrade \-\-check" ,
and the
.B "system layout"
commands).
For
.B status
and the
.B assets
commands, a library the package ships did not load; the enabled agents,
a directory of the view,
.IR operator.conf ,
or the type of an entry a row is about could not be read; or the rows could
not be put in order.
The page names the reading it could not make.
Each is an exit status
.BR ai-tools-records (5)
states.
Nothing was changed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AI_TOOLS_ASSUME_YES
Set to
.B 1
to answer every question whose default is already
.I yes
without prompting, for unattended runs.
A question defaulting to
.I no
still prompts, so this never turns a refusal into consent.
.TP
.B AI_TOOLS_NVM_VERSION
Pin the nvm release
.B "system bootstrap"
installs
.RI ( vX.Y.Z ).
Unset, it installs the latest release, resolved at run time.
.TP
.B AI_TOOLS_NODE_MAJOR
The Node major version
.B "system bootstrap"
installs.
Defaults to 22.
.TP
.B AI_TOOLS_SERVICE_LIVE_TIMEOUT
The whole seconds
.B status
waits for an answer from the sandbox account's
.B "systemd \-\-user"
manager before it reads that account's units from their last-run stamp
instead.
Defaults to 5.
A value that is not a positive whole number leaves each of those units to its
stamp.
.PP
Host settings are not environment variables: they live in
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5),
which is root-owned so that a session cannot widen what it is given.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ai-tools/operator.conf
Operator registry and host options.
.B operators
edits the
.B OPERATORS
line in place and leaves every other setting as written.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects
The enrolled account's own allowlist of project paths, seeded by
.BR "operators add" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/secret-patterns
The same account's secret\-name patterns, seeded by
.BR "operators add" .
A pattern listed here replaces the built\-in baseline in
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/secret-patterns.lib.sh
rather than adding to it; the seeded file lists none, so the baseline applies.
.TP
.I /opt/ai-tools
The sandbox account's home, holding the nvm tree, the installed agents and the
launchers
.B "system bootstrap"
points at them.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/assets-bindings.d
.TQ
.I /etc/ai-tools/assets-bindings.d
The shipped bindings and yours: which key may sign each set name.
.B "assets enable"
accepts an asset only of a set one of them names;
.BR ai-tools-assets (5)
has the file.
.TP
.I /opt/ai-tools/skills
.TQ
.I /opt/ai-tools/subagents
The view: one link per linked asset, which
.B "assets reconcile"
keeps, beside the copies the package seeds there.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/admin-commands.d
The command domains installed provider packages contribute, one executable per
domain, named for the provider.
.TP
.I /usr/share/selinux/packages/ai-tools
The compiled policy modules
.B "selinux groups enable"
loads.
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/ai-tools
The grant that lets an operator launch a sandboxed session.
.B "system post-upgrade"
shows changes to it and never writes it.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/bash-completion/completions/ai-tools-admin
A link to the bash completion
.BR ai-tools (1)
describes, which completes
.B ai-tools-admin
and
.B atr admin
alike, with the domains this host's provider packages contribute among the
commands.
.SH EXAMPLES
Bring a freshly installed host up: provision the toolchain, then enrol
yourself.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr admin system bootstrap
atr admin operators add "$(id \-un)"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Provision a host unattended, enabling one agent without the menu:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr admin system bootstrap \-\-agents agent\-codex
.EE
.RE
.PP
Provision a second agent after the first run \(em add it, as
.BI agent\- NAME\fR,\fP
to
.B AI_TOOLS_AGENTS
in
.BR ai-tools-operator.conf (5),
then re-run the same command:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr admin system bootstrap
.EE
.RE
.PP
Provision the toolchain and every integration enabled on this host, then see
what the .NET one has:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr admin system bootstrap \-\-scope full
atr admin dotnet status
.EE
.RE
.PP
Claim a project for a passwordless service account you enrolled earlier:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr admin operators add svc\-agent
atr projects claim \-\-for svc\-agent ~/src/myproject
.EE
.RE
.PP
See which policy groups are loaded, then widen the domain for a .NET build:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr admin selinux groups
atr admin selinux groups enable tmpmap
.EE
.RE
.PP
Reconcile the configuration files an upgrade left behind:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr admin system post\-upgrade
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check each morning from root's crontab, with a mail only when a file needs
you; the script keeps the stream when the exit says the reading found
something or could not complete:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
0 7 * * * /usr/sbin/ai\-tools\-admin system post\-upgrade \-\-check
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr admin system post\-upgrade \-\-check > /var/tmp/post\-upgrade.tsv
case $? in
    0) rm \-f /var/tmp/post\-upgrade.tsv ;;
    4) logger \-t post\-upgrade "findings left; see the stream" ;;
    5) logger \-t post\-upgrade "reading incomplete; see the stream" ;;
    *) logger \-t post\-upgrade "the command failed" ;;
esac
.EE
.RE
.PP
Put the agent entrypoints back in order after a toolchain update, when a launch
refuses over the label:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr admin system entrypoints relabel
.EE
.RE
